From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 05:17:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] service: expose the access point behind a WiFi service

ConnMan already knows all of this. gsupplicant keeps a struct
g_supplicant_bss per BSS with the BSSID, frequency, maximum rate and
cipher suites it read from wpa_supplicant, and every network points at
the one it is currently represented by through best_bss. None of it is
published, so a client can be associated to an access point and still be
unable to say which one, on what channel, at what rate.

Adds BSSID, Frequency, MaxRate and EncryptionMode to net.connman.Service
for WiFi services, plumbed the way the existing WiFi values already are:
accessors on gsupplicant, the WiFi plugin copying them onto the network
next to the frequency it already sets, and the service mirroring them
the same place it mirrors the WPS values. Frequency needed no new
plumbing below the service - connman_network_set_frequency() has always
been called, the value simply stopped there.

Each property is announced individually when it changes, and all four
are omitted rather than published empty when the supplicant has not
reported a BSS: a service that has only ever been seen in a scan result
may have none backing it.

gsupplicant gains a "BSS" network-changed notification, emitted when
best_bss is replaced. The existing "Signal" notification cannot stand in
for it because it is conditional on the strength differing, so a roam to
an access point reading the same strength would leave the old BSSID
published.

Note this is deliberately the same set of properties Sailfish's ConnMan
fork exposes and libconnman-qt already binds to, so clients written
against that need no changes. Channel width is not included: it is not
in the BSS properties wpa_supplicant sends, and would mean parsing the
HT/VHT/HE operation elements out of the IEs.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Pending
